---
// Lesson 7.3a — Ladders I: The Climbing State
// isOnLadder + a third movement mode; vertical input while climbing.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.3a — Ladders I: The Climbing State</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Walk. Jump. Climb. Your player is about to grow a third movement
    mode — one that turns gravity OFF, reads a new input axis, and
    exists only because a trigger said so. Ladders are your second
    full state machine, and you already know every pattern it needs.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>The design, before any code — four states of the body:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>State</th><th>Gravity</th><th>Controls</th><th>Animation</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Top-down (overworld)</td><td>0</td><td>WASD translate</td><td>WalkDir</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Grounded (platformer)</td><td>2</td><td>A/D translate, Space jump</td><td>IsWalking + jump</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Climbing (new)</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td><strong>W/S translate</strong></td><td><strong>climb/idle-on-ladder</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Airborne</td><td>2</td><td>(no control — physics)</td><td>jump/fall</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Predict: climbing turns gravityScale to 0 — the same number
        the overworld uses in Start (<a href="5.3a.html">5.3a</a>).
        Why does the SAME zero mean two different things there and
        here? And why must vertical input SLOW the climb (speed 3,
        not 5)? When do you want a ladder to feel deliberate?
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The ladder changes state via a TRIGGER enter/exit pair —
            OnTriggerEnter2D sets isOnLadder, OnTriggerExit2D clears
            it. Compare that with the lever's remembered toggle
            (<a href="5.2a.html">5.2a</a>): why is a zone remembered
            only while you are IN it, but a lever remembered
            forever? What does each design assume about the player's
            intent?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the zone.</strong> Build a ladder: a tall
    trigger collider (Is Trigger checked!) tagged "Ladder" (make the
    tag — <a href="2.3a.html">2.3a</a> ritual) plus whatever ladder
    art you have in the starter sprites. The collider IS the ladder;
    the art is a costume.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the state fields.</strong> In
    PlayerController, same voice as always:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float climbSpeed = 3f;
private bool isOnLadder = false;

private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
{
    if (other.CompareTag("Ladder")) isOnLadder = true;
}

private void OnTriggerExit2D(Collider2D other)
{
    if (other.CompareTag("Ladder")) isOnLadder = false;
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the climb movement.</strong> In Update,
    INSIDE the isPlatformer branch (ladders are cave-only!), add a
    climbing lane:</p>
    <pre><code>if (isOnLadder)
{
    rb.gravityScale = 0;                 // the ladder owns gravity
    float vDirection = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");
    Vector2 vVector = new Vector2(0, vDirection * climbSpeed * Time.deltaTime);
    transform.Translate(vVector);

    if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space)) // jump OFF the ladder
    {
        isOnLadder = false;  // ...but the exit trigger will not fire!
        rb.gravityScale = 2;
        rb.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
    }
}
else
{
    rb.gravityScale = 2;                // normal platformer gravity
    // ... your existing move/jump code runs here
}</code></pre>
    <p>Press Play and climb. You are reading the SAME Vertical axis
    the overworld uses — one input, three meanings, chosen by state.
    That is your whole movement course in one if.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — the exit bug you must meet.</strong> Read the
    comment in the jump-off code. When you Space off the ladder,
    you set isOnLadder = false by hand — but if you jumped off but
    your BODY is still touching the trigger, the NEXT
    OnTriggerEnter2D does not fire (you never exited!), and… you
    land and cannot re-climb without stepping out and back in.
    Fix: keep a one-frame grace or check the trigger each frame.
    Bugs like this are why trigger state pairs deserve a test
    ritual.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The sticky ladder</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Forget the <code>else</code> that restores
                gravityScale to 2. What happens to the FIRST jump
                after climbing — and why does the symptom appear
                far from the cause?</li>
            <li>Set climbSpeed to your xSpeed (5). Climb a ladder
                next to a wall. Why does "responsive" read as
                "twitchy" — and what did the slower number
                buy?</li>
            <li>Leave gravity at 2 while climbing. What happens at
                the exact moment you stop pressing W — and why is
                "sliding down ladders" a REAL GAME FEATURE you
                just discovered by accident?</li>
            <li>Your ladder trigger is 1 tile wide; your player is
                1.2 tiles. Describe the exit/enter flicker at the
                seam — which two Unity messages are firing per
                frame?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Climb design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Snap: when you enter a ladder's zone horizontally,
                your x should LOCK to the ladder's x. What would
                read that value — other.transform.position.x —
                and where would you store it?</li>
            <li>Design a ladder that is a RISK: spikes at the top on
                one side. What does climbing toward danger do to
                input speed's meaning?</li>
            <li>Hard: climb with an ANIMATION variant (the starter
                animator may have climb frames — check). Which
                parameter do you add — and which branch sets
                it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Build a trigger-based ladder zone with its own tag</li>
            <li>Explain climb as a state: gravity 0, vertical input, else restores</li>
            <li>Demonstrate and fix the jump-off re-entry bug</li>
            <li>Explain one input axis serving three meanings via state</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — the ladder reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — states all the way down</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.4a.html">7.4a — Ladders II: Mounts, Tops &amp; Polish</a></p>
</div>`;
